WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:01.034
History and

00:00:01.034 --> 00:00:02.135
current polling both

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:04.104
tell us that the House of Representatives

00:00:04.104 --> 00:00:05.005
will likely slip

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:06.840
over to Republican control

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.842
in the November midterms.

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.477
What happens then?

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:11.578
Actual governance

00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:13.146
will come to a standstill.

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:15.181
There will be a flurry of investigations

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:16.182
on everything

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:17.550
from the Justice Department

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.686
to Hunter Biden to the border crisis

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.287
The January six committee

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:23.523
will almost certainly be disbanded.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:24.791
And it is not implausible

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:25.592
to imagine

00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:27.927
that President Biden will be impeached.

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:31.197
How did we get here?

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:33.133
There are, of course, many reasons,

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:35.301
but the central facilitating factor

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:36.469
is surely the way

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:38.671
that American politics has over

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.306
the last few decades

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:41.875
increasingly empowered

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:44.077
the extremes of political parties

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:47.047
at the expense of the mainstream.

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:48.181
The primary system

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:49.582
used by American parties

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:51.217
to choose their candidates

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.119
is extremely unusual.

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:55.488
No other major democracy has won

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:56.756
quite like it.

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:57.390
Primaries

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.591
ensured that candidates

00:00:58.591 --> 00:00:59.659
chosen are selected

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:01.561
by a sliver of the party

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:04.064
around 20% of voters,

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:05.331
and the selection is not

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:06.900
at all representative.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.268
These are the most intense

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:10.370
agitated activists, often

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.405
far more extreme in their views

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:13.406
than run of the mill

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.942
registered Republicans or Democrats.

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:17.243
Add to this decades

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:19.112
of sophisticated computer

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:20.880
enabled gerrymandering,

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.882
and you get extreme candidates

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:24.551
who run in safe districts

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:26.319
where the only threat to them

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:27.854
is a primary candidate

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:30.090
who is even more extreme.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.624
The Washington Post has analyzed

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:33.560
Republicans running for Senate, House

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.095
and certain statewide offices

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:36.863
and found that a majority

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:39.766
could be classified as election deniers.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.168
People who have in some way questioned,

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:43.369
challenged or refused

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:44.204
to accept

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:45.772
the outcome of the 2020

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:47.607
presidential election.

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:50.477
Of these 291 candidates,

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.880
171 are running in safe

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.949
Republican districts.

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:58.351
So what began as a fringe theory

00:01:58.351 --> 00:02:00.320
promoted by Donald Trump,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.888
but initially rejected

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:03.857
by most of the Republican Party's

00:02:03.857 --> 00:02:06.860
leaders, has now become the majority

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:07.861
view of the party.

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:09.462
Election

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:11.164
denial is not a majority view

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:12.732
in the United States.

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:16.269
In an NBC poll, 57% of those asked said

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:18.371
they would be less likely to vote

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:19.239
for someone

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:20.607
who claims Trump won

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:24.210
the 2020 election, while only 21% said

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.745
they would be more likely

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:28.148
to support an election denial.

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:28.848
But between

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:30.383
primaries and gerrymandering,

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:32.585
the majority view gets drowned out.

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:34.754
Catering to the right wing base

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:37.023
also means constantly ratcheting up

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:37.991
the rhetoric.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.393
Nancy Pelosi is a would be dictator.

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:42.295
Biden is a communist.

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:44.597
Democrats are pro criminals.

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:46.866
Marjorie Taylor Greene has said, quote,

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:49.369
Democrats want Republicans dead

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.371
and they have already started

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:53.406
the killings, unquote.

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:55.341
The alternative system of candidates

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:56.709
election used in America

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:58.411
before the age of primaries

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:00.713
and in most other major democracies,

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:01.748
is what is often called

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:03.016
a smoke filled room,

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:04.417
a pejorative description.

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:05.518
Even before we knew

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:07.420
that smoking killed you

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.221
in this system,

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:10.623
candidates are selected by party bosses.

00:03:10.924 --> 00:03:11.691
But consider who

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:13.793
these bosses have traditionally been

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:15.461
aldermen, mayors,

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:17.764
governors and legislators at all levels.

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:19.899
These are people who have won

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:21.267
general elections

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:23.636
by appealing to the entire electorate.

00:03:23.937 --> 00:03:25.171
People who have a feel

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:26.940
for the broader public.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:28.041
No group of party

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:28.741
elders, for

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:30.210
example, would ever choose

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.645
a candidate like Herschel Walker.

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:33.980
Primaries, by contrast,

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.215
entrust candidates

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:37.250
election to the most radical section

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:38.351
of the party,

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:39.352
and social media

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:40.753
has added fuel to the fire

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.722
by amplifying the noisiest

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.824
and angriest voices within the party.

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:47.227
Who are themselves an even smaller group

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:48.361
than primary voters.

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:51.197
While the problem is far worse

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:52.532
and much more dangerous

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.100
on the Republican side,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.803
this pressures do also affect Democrats.

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:58.204
Many of the issues

00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:00.106
where Joe Biden is constrained

00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:01.307
in his actions,

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:03.743
in particular immigration and energy

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:04.577
are ones

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:06.679
where the activist base of the party

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:08.815
has much more extreme views

00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:10.316
than the mainstream.

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:11.751
And pivoting to the center

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:12.652
as Pennsylvania

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:13.453
Senate candidate

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:14.687
John Fetterman did on

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:16.389
fracking in recent months

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:17.690
is increasingly difficult

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:18.758
in today's world,

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:20.493
where you can instantly and easily

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:22.495
play old clips of a politician.

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:25.231
Before he changed his mind.

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:26.099
In a recent piece

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:27.400
in The New York Times, Max

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.301
Fisher describes

00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:29.702
how the recent dysfunctions

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:31.204
of British politics

00:04:31.204 --> 00:04:33.773
can be attributed to the two main parties

00:04:34.007 --> 00:04:36.309
choosing over the last two decades

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:39.145
to adopt more of a primary type system

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:40.480
to select their leader.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.081
The Labor Party ended up with the

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:44.684
totally unelectable far left leader

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:46.152
like Jeremy Corbyn

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:48.354
and rejected a charismatic moderate

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.189
like David Miliband.

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:51.424
The recent Conservative

00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:52.825
Party travails illustrate

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:53.293
the problem

00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:55.461
perfectly mistrusts with her

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.363
totally impractical warmed

00:04:57.363 --> 00:04:58.731
over Thatcherism.

00:04:58.731 --> 00:04:59.799
Almost always came

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:00.967
third in votes

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:03.002
from elected members of parliament.

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:05.338
The old system of party bosses.

00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:07.540
But she was the darling of the broader

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:08.474
party membership,

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:10.643
which is highly unrepresentative

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:12.011
of the British public.

00:05:12.011 --> 00:05:13.446
And they were the ones

00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:15.715
who made the final decision.

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:16.983
It is not an accident

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:18.685
that Germany and France

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:20.420
have both been run largely

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.356
by solid centrists in a time of populism.

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:25.625
They have chosen to keep

00:05:25.625 --> 00:05:27.894
to the old system of democracy

00:05:27.894 --> 00:05:30.363
based on the principle of majority rule

00:05:30.897 --> 00:05:33.099
in America and to an extent in Britain.

00:05:33.533 --> 00:05:35.368
Democracy has actually become

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:36.969
a minority rule,

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:39.138
and the minority holding power

00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:40.740
is unrepresentative.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.109
Angry and increasingly radical.

